Here the DP is filled from right to left. `size[i]` is the longest chain that starts with `nums[i]`, and `next[i]` points to the next number in that chain. Checking `nums[j] % nums[i]` is enough because the array is sorted.

For an input with no divisibility links, every `size` value stays `1`, so returning any single number is valid. Sorting changes the input slice in place.

```go
import "sort"

func largestDivisibleSubset(nums []int) []int {
	if len(nums) == 0 {
		return []int{}
	}

	sort.Ints(nums)
	n := len(nums)
	size := make([]int, n)
	next := make([]int, n)

	bestIndex := 0
	bestSize := 1

	for i := n - 1; i >= 0; i-- {
		size[i] = 1
		next[i] = -1

		for j := i + 1; j < n; j++ {
			if nums[j]%nums[i] == 0 && size[i] < size[j]+1 {
				size[i] = size[j] + 1
				next[i] = j
			}
		}

		if size[i] > bestSize {
			bestSize = size[i]
			bestIndex = i
		}
	}

	answer := make([]int, 0, bestSize)
	for index := bestIndex; index != -1; index = next[index] {
		answer = append(answer, nums[index])
	}
	return answer
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` after the `O(n log n)` sort.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

The `length` array is enough to rebuild a chain if we scan backward. First, sorting and DP find the longest chain ending at each position. During the reverse scan, a number belongs to the answer when it divides the last selected number and its saved length is exactly one less than the current target.

Dry run: for `[1, 2, 4]`, the lengths are `[1, 2, 3]`. Starting from `4`, the scan picks `2`, then `1`, producing the answer in reverse order before reversing it.

```javascript
class Solution {
    largestDivisibleSubset(nums) {
        if (nums.length === 0) {
            return [];
        }

        nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
        const n = nums.length;
        const length = new Array(n).fill(1);

        let bestLength = 1;
        let bestIndex = 0;

        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[i] % nums[j] === 0) {
                    length[i] = Math.max(length[i], length[j] + 1);
                }
            }

            if (length[i] > bestLength) {
                bestLength = length[i];
                bestIndex = i;
            }
        }

        const answer = [];
        let lastValue = nums[bestIndex];
        let neededLength = bestLength;

        for (let i = bestIndex; i >= 0 && neededLength > 0; i--) {
            if (lastValue % nums[i] === 0 && length[i] === neededLength) {
                answer.push(nums[i]);
                lastValue = nums[i];
                neededLength--;
            }
        }

        answer.reverse();
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for the pair checks, plus `O(n log n)` for sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the DP array and returned subset. The input array is sorted in place.

This keeps an actual subset for every position instead of storing parent indexes. After sorting, `best[i]` is the longest valid subset whose largest value is `nums[i]`. When a smaller value divides `nums[i]`, its saved list can be extended.

A small dry run with `[3, 6, 12]` gives `[3]`, then `[3, 6]`, then `[3, 6, 12]`. The code does not change the caller's list because it copies it before sorting.

```python
class Solution:
    def largestDivisibleSubset(self, nums):
        if not nums:
            return []

        values = sorted(nums)
        best = [[value] for value in values]

        for i in range(len(values)):
            for j in range(i):
                if values[i] % values[j] == 0:
                    candidate = best[j] + [values[i]]
                    if len(candidate) > len(best[i]):
                        best[i] = candidate

        answer = best[0]
        for subset in best[1:]:
            if len(subset) > len(answer):
                answer = subset
        return answer
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` comparisons, with `O(n log n)` sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` in the general case because many saved subsets contain lists.

Sorting makes the divisibility check one-way. For each `nums[i]`, I store the longest valid chain that ends there. The `previous` array remembers where that chain came from, so the answer can be rebuilt at the end.

The input array is sorted in place. For example, after sorting `[1, 8, 4, 2]` becomes `[1, 2, 4, 8]`, and the chain found is `1 -> 2 -> 4 -> 8`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> largestDivisibleSubset(vector<int> nums) {
        if (nums.empty()) {
            return {};
        }

        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> length(n, 1);
        vector<int> previous(n, -1);

        int bestIndex = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                if (nums[i] % nums[j] == 0 &&
                    length[i] < length[j] + 1) {
                    length[i] = length[j] + 1;
                    previous[i] = j;
                }
            }

            if (length[i] > length[bestIndex]) {
                bestIndex = i;
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        while (bestIndex != -1) {
            answer.push_back(nums[bestIndex]);
            bestIndex = previous[bestIndex];
        }

        reverse(answer.begin(), answer.end());
        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` after sorting, with sorting adding `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

I used recursion where `solve(index)` means the best subset starting at `index`. Since the numbers are sorted, the next number only needs to be a multiple of the current one. The memo array avoids solving the same index again.

The result is built while returning from recursion. An empty input is handled before accessing the first element.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    private int[] values;
    private int[] memo;
    private int[] nextIndex;

    public List<Integer> largestDivisibleSubset(int[] nums) {
        if (nums == null || nums.length == 0) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        Arrays.sort(nums);
        int n = nums.length;
        values = nums;
        memo = new int[n];
        nextIndex = new int[n];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        Arrays.fill(nextIndex, -1);

        int start = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            if (solve(i) > solve(start)) {
                start = i;
            }
        }

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        while (start != -1) {
            answer.add(values[start]);
            start = nextIndex[start];
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private int solve(int index) {
        if (memo[index] != -1) {
            return memo[index];
        }

        int bestLength = 1;
        int bestNext = -1;

        for (int next = index + 1; next < values.length; ++next) {
            if (values[next] % values[index] == 0) {
                int candidate = 1 + solve(next);
                if (candidate > bestLength) {
                    bestLength = candidate;
                    bestNext = next;
                }
            }
        }

        nextIndex[index] = bestNext;
        memo[index] = bestLength;
        return bestLength;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` plus `O(n log n)` for sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the memo arrays and recursion stack.
